136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (           Tea is a major agricultural crop in Sri Lanka and makes an important contribution to the national economy through export income, employment, and rural development. Sri Lanka is well known worldwide for producing high-quality Ceylon tea.Tea harvesting is one of the most labour-intensive tasks in tea production, as plucking must be carried out regularly while maintaining strict quality standards. Traditionally, tea leaves are harvested manually by skilled workers who carefully select tender shoots to ensure better quality and future plant growth.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (However, the tea industry is currently facing major challenges such as labour shortages, increasing wages, and high production costs, making manual plucking less sustainable. To address these issues, mechanical tea harvesting systems such as handheld shears, motorized harvesters, and portable tea plucking machines have been introduced to improve efficiency and reduce dependence on labour. Although these technologies can increase harvesting speed, they also create problems including leaf damage, poor shoot selection, inclusion of coarse material, and operational difficulties in uneven field conditions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (A major challenge in mechanical tea harvesting is maintaining the required plucking standard. In tea cultivation, the harvestable portion is called the pluckable shoot, which generally consists of a bud with two or three tender leaves. The plucking standard varies depending on climatic conditions and plantation management practices. During wet seasons, rapid plant growth allows comparatively harder plucking standards such as three leaves and a bud, while during dry seasons lighter plucking standards such as two leaves and a bud are preferred to maintain tea quality. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (                      Understanding tea shoot structure is essential when analyzing mechanical tea harvesting systems because harvesting efficiency and quality depend on correctly identifying pluckable shoots. A tea shoot consists of the apical bud, which is the actively growing terminal bud responsible for shoot elongation, followed by progressively developing young leaves. Near the shoot base, the first immature protective leaf is called the fish leaf, while the mature supporting leaf left after harvesting is known as the mother leaf. Very young immature shoots not ready for harvesting are locally called Arimbu and must remain until reaching the required plucking stage. Tea buds can be classified as active buds and dormant buds, depending on growth conditions, while some shoots may enter a temporary resting condition called banji, where the apical bud becomes inactive and shoot growth stops, making them unsuitable as ideal pluckable shoots. After harvesting, new shoots regenerate from remaining buds, with growth influenced by temperature, rainfall, sunlight, and soil moisture. Tea bushes are maintained at a uniform height known as the plucking table, and the time interval between two harvesting cycles is called the plucking round, both of which are important for maintaining sustainable yield and tea quality.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
