/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
153 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Figure 03:  Field Observation at TRI Showing Tea Bush Damage and Immature Shoot Loss Caused by Existing Mechanical Harvesting Systems)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (Another major problem is the difficulty of operating mechanical harvesters on uneven terrain. Tea plantations in Sri Lanka are commonly located on steep hillsides and irregular land surfaces. Many available machines are designed for relatively flat tea fields found in temperate regions and therefore cannot maintain a stable cutting height under local plantation conditions. This results in inconsistent plucking, increased leaf damage, operator discomfort, and lower harvesting accuracy. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (In addition, differences between tropical and temperate tea-growing conditions significantly affect machine performance. Due to faster shoot growth in tropical climates, Sri Lankan tea bushes require shorter plucking rounds and more accurate harvesting control. Machines developed for temperate regions may not satisfy these requirements because they are designed for different shoot densities, growth patterns, and harvesting intervals. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E ( Therefore, this project is necessary to conduct a detailed evaluation of currently available tea harvesting systems and identify their limitations under Sri Lankan plantation conditions. The findings of this study can contribute toward the development of more suitable harvesting solutions capable of improving harvesting efficiency while maintaining tea quality standards. The project will also provide engineering recommendations for future design improvements focusing on selective plucking capability, terrain adaptability, ergonomic operation, and reduced leaf damage.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
