/E (Reduced Harvesting Cost: Mechanized harvesting can significantly reduce labour dependency and lower harvesting costs compared to manual plucking\(manual harvesting costs approximately Rs. 375 per kg of made tea\), improving overall estate profitability.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 450 0 R
/E (Improved Tea Market Value: Selective harvesting of pluckable shoots is expected to reduce leaf damage, coarse leaf content, and refuse tea percentages, thereby improving final tea quality and market competitiveness.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 451 0 R
/E (Lower Post-Harvest Processing Cost: Precise harvesting minimizes the collection of non-pluckable shoots, reducing sorting requirements and processing losses.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 452 0 R
/E (Local Adaptability: Existing harvesting machines are mainly designed for temperate tea-growing regions and show poor adaptability to Sri Lankan terrain and plucking standards. The proposed system introduces a solution specifically adapted to local plantation conditions.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (5.2 Social Considerations)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (           The proposed system also contributes to workforce safety, labour productivity, and modernization of plantation operations.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 453 0 R
/E (Improved Occupational Safety: The mechanized system reduces repetitive finger strain, hand injuries, and musculoskeletal fatigue associated with manual plucking.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
